  "body": "I'm writing tests for dynamic import(), and am struck by how setTimeout() behaves so differently, per spec, from eval/inline event handlers/other ways of evaluating strings.\r\n\r\nPer spec right now, it gets the document's base URL and the [default classic script fetch options](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#default-classic-script-fetch-options). This leads to weird consequences, like\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!-- index.html -->\r\n\r\n<script src=\"scripts/eval.js\"></script>\r\n<script src=\"scripts/setTimeout.js\"></script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// scripts/eval.js\r\neval(`import('./foo.js')`);\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// scripts/setTimeout.js\r\nsetTimeout(`import('./foo.js')`, 0);\r\n```\r\n\r\nloading both `scripts/foo.js` and `foo.js`. This seems clearly wrong, and I can just fix it, since I introduced it.\r\n\r\nBut this also applies to the fetch options, which have always been \"clean-slate\" ever since @mikewest started integrating CSP into HTML. That is, instead of inheriting any cryptographic nonce or parser metadata, they get empty string/non-parser-inserted.\r\n\r\nI also introduced the credentials mode being reset to \"omit\", but am thinking maybe that should inherit too.\r\n\r\nI'll put out a patch for the base URL (and maybe credentials mode?) but wanted to get @mikewest's take on the cryptographic nonce and parser metadata.\r\n\r\n/cc @whatwg/modules ",
